WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.919
One of the questions I see most often in

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.279
my messages is, "Chris, I want to start

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:06.640
a business or I want to buy a business,

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.320
but I don't have any money." And you

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.080
know what I would say to that? Good.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.599
Because constraints equal creativity.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.519
And that's what this whole episode today

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:15.280
is about. I'm going to call it how to

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:17.119
buy big things with little money.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.880
Whether that's a piece of real estate,

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:20.960
maybe you're buying a business, maybe

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.880
you're buying equipment or assets needed

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.640
to start a business, maybe you're trying

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.119
to buy equity for a business that you

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.119
work for, you want to buy a franchise. I

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:31.359
don't care. This episode is where you

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:33.120
will go to learn how to buy big things

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:36.160
with little money. And I would know

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.920
because A, I grew up poor. B, I'm cheap.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.000
I don't want to pay full price. And if I

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.399
do a whole bunch of research and I come

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.640
to the conclusion that this initiative

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:46.239
that I'm looking at is not as risky as

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.000
it looks from the outside, then I'm

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:50.000
going to be willing to take some risk to

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.039
make sure that I get what I want. So, if

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
you're looking for an episode where you

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:53.520
talk about, oh, have you heard of

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.800
Kickstarter? Have you heard of seller

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.640
financing? No. This isn't going to be

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.320
generic. This is going to be creative

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.840
and it's going to be very tactical. And

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.680
everything I'm about to tell you, I

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.680
actually have experience with, guys.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.680
I've been buying real estate for 17

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.600
years. I've been buying RV parks and

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.680
mobile home parks for the last 7 years.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.560
I bought an $800,000 business for

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.400
$50,000 down. I invested in my best

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.640
friend's business that was worth 2.2

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.560
million. He only put 10% down and he

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.040
financed almost all of that from friends

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.119
and family. I have been in the weeds of

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.640
buying big things with little money my

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:26.320
entire career. So, if you're looking for

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.560
just a video where I just regurgitate

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.880
things that ChatGpt tells me and go

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.880
somewhere else.

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:35.759
Before I get into these tactics, I want

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:37.280
to tell a couple stories to illustrate

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:38.960
where I'm coming from here. When I was

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.479
21 years old, I had just spent the

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.040
summer reading books about

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.200
entrepreneurship and real estate. And I

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.119
had moved to Alabama. I just got

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.399
married. And I was fired up to make

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:50.640
something happen, to start a business,

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:52.960
to buy real estate, whatever. Turns out

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.880
2008 was not a great time to buy real

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.640
estate, especially if you are a broke

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.880
college student with student loans

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.119
waiting tables for 213 an hour plus

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.880
tips. But sometimes ignorance is a

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:04.960
superpower. And my wife and I started

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.159
looking on Zillow. We found a house,

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.039
this house right here, if you're

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:12.640
watching, 1805 21st Avenue East, and it

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:15.280
was $90,000. And we bought it for 3.5%

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.879
down. We used grants from the government

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.200
to pay for it, plus a little bit of

00:02:16.879 --> 00:02:20.959
savings from our serving job at a Thai

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.959
restaurant, and we bought something big

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:24.720
with little money. And then President

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:26.480
Obama had a first-time home buyer tax

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.440
credit, which basically meant you got

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.360
$8,000 cash after you bought your first

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.440
home. And so about 9 months later, we

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.760
got a check in the mail for $8,000. And

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.840
we put that towards a home about 5

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.440
minutes away, our first rental home. We

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.680
were still broke college students, still

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.519
making 213 an hour, but we owned two

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.360
homes. We just kept talking to bankers

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:47.040
until we found one that said yes. So, we

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.319
finally found a bank that said yes, but

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.440
they said, "Chris, you're going to have

00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:52.720
to pay PMI." What is PMI? It's mortgage

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.879
insurance. It's going to bring your

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.280
payment from $535

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:59.440
to $700 a month. Now, as a broke college

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.519
student, that extra 160 bucks a month

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.800
was just a non-starter. So, I said,

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:03.920
"Well, I don't I don't want to pay that.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.000
How can I get around that?" Well, you

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.840
can't. You have to pay 20% down if you

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.680
don't want to pay PMI. And I was just

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.080
like, "No." I read that I don't have to

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.200
pay that. and they were like, "Uh, all

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.319
right. I don't think it's going to work,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.239
but let me see if there are other

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:18.080
options." Guess what? Magically, there

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:20.400
were other options. I got to pay three

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.480
and a half% down. I didn't have to pay

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.120
PMI because they had to choose between

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.360
that and losing my business entirely.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.120
And in the wake of the financial crash,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.000
I'm sure they were thrilled to even have

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.200
this tiny $90,000 mortgage. That was one

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.799
thing that I remembered from all those

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.239
books that I read at Barnes & Noble.

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:40.799
Many loans require you to have PMI, but

00:03:38.239 --> 00:03:42.480
almost all banks have other loan options

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:44.080
that don't require that. They don't want

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.680
to give you the option of those loan

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.280
packages because it's not as profitable

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.959
to them. But if they have to choose

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.799
between losing your business and giving

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:52.239
you a less profitable loan, they're

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:53.920
going to give you the loan you want. I

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:56.000
ended up owning that house for 11 years

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.920
and renting it for nine of those 11.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.200
Fast forward about 8 years and we were

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.640
in the middle of building our dream

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.159
house with a custom home builder, the

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.599
one I'm sitting in right now. And I'll

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:05.200
never forget, I was getting this office

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:07.200
designed and they had already laid the

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.720
foundation. I think the drywall was even

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.959
up and they were about to lay carpet.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.640
And I realized at the last minute that I

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:14.560
wanted a floor plug right in the middle

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.920
of the room because I knew my desk would

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.079
be right here where I'm sitting today

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.519
and I wanted to plug my MacBook in in

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:20.959
the floor. So I went to the builder and

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:22.320
I said, "Hey, is it too late to add a

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:24.479
floor plug? Cuz like doesn't that need

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.080
to go in the foundation or something? I

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:30.080
don't know how it works." and he said to

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.000
me, "We can reverse, change, fix, add,

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.840
anything at any stage in the build

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
process. Literally, anything can be

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.680
fixed or changed. The only question is

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.520
how much are you willing to pay?" And

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.320
for whatever reason, that line stuck

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.240
with me. Everything is figure outable.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.320
Everything. Whether it's buying a home

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.639
on 213 an hour as a broke college

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.720
student with no assets to your name or

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:52.160
adding a simple floor plug at the point

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.080
your house is almost finished.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.759
Constraints equal creativity. Now just

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.720
one more analogy before I get into the

00:04:55.759 --> 00:05:00.800
tactics. Vexalology is the study of

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.360
flags. Now when a flag designer is

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.520
designing a flag, they have to start

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.039
designing it on an index card. They have

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.600
to shrink it down all the way to an

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:11.120
index card. Because if your flag doesn't

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.560
work or if it doesn't look good when

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.320
shrunk down to the size of an index

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.639
card, then it's not going to look good

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.400
as a flag. It has to make sense in 1

00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:20.240
second. It has to look good while

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.240
flapping in the wind. It has to be

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.160
stitched and not printed. It has to be

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.479
drawn by a child. And it has to be

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.039
recognizable from far away. So, we're

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:31.440
not doing tiny details or gradients or

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:33.360
clever explanations. It's got to be

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.360
simple. Shout out to my friend Bill for

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.360
that analogy. So, trying to buy

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.600
something that's very expensive or

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:41.840
invest in something when you have no

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.520
money or almost no money is the index

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.520
card. You can't rely on big down

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.759
payments or perfect credit or standard

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.000
financing terms. You're forced to ask

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:52.560
better questions. What does the seller

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.479
actually want? What part of this deal is

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.639
flexible? What part is negotiable? What

00:05:54.479 --> 00:05:58.800
part isn't? Instead of adding cash, can

00:05:56.639 --> 00:06:01.120
I just remove some risk? Can I change

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.199
the timeline instead of the price? Can I

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.639
change the terms instead of the price?

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:06.880
You've probably heard the phrase, my

00:06:04.639 --> 00:06:08.639
price, your terms, or your price, my

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.160
terms. That's just a small part of what

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:13.759
we're talking about here. And this is

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:17.120
why I am so adamantly against raising

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:19.440
money for your startup in 99% of cases

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.440
because it removes the need to be

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.919
creative. That's why venture-funded

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.919
startups statistically have no better

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:27.520
chance of succeeding than bootstrap

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:28.720
startups. Because once you get a few

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.160
million bucks in the bank, you don't

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.520
have to be creative. Let's hire a

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.960
marketing guy. Oh, we need our profit

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.720
and loss statement. Yeah, let's hire a

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.400
CFO. All right, so let's start covering

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.720
these tactics. And I'm going to save the

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.639
very best for last. Number one, do it

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.800
publicly. A while back, I had an episode

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:45.120
with my friend Isaac French, and he

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.280
built this property called Live Oak Lake

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.360
just outside of Waco, Texas. It's I

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.360
think seven beautiful cabins. They're

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.280
Scandinavian style. He bought kits. He

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.759
shipped them overseas. He dug out a

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.600
pond. This property was like 5 to 10

00:06:55.759 --> 00:07:00.000
acres and it had these mature oak trees

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.880
on it. Long story short, 2 years after

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.599
building it, he sold it for 7 or $8

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.840
million. And his costs on all of it was

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:09.759
like 1 and a half or 2 million. So, one

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.240
deal that took two to three years of his

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:14.160
life provided him life-changing money.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.080
Enough money for him to retire. And he

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.759
had very, very, very little money to put

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.360
in. Guys, if you're liking this so far,

00:07:17.759 --> 00:07:20.800
please hit subscribe or follow or

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.919
whatever. Share with a friend. It would

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.840
really mean a lot. Now, if you want to

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:26.080
hear that story, I'll link to it below.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.840
But this tactic I'm telling you about is

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.360
actually from one of his friends that

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.199
wanted to do something similar. They

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.280
wanted to build these beautiful

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:35.520
short-term rentals, but they didn't have

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.599
any money. So, what did they do? They

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.039
started an Instagram account with zero

00:07:37.599 --> 00:07:40.479
followers, and they just started

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:42.800
documenting their process with their

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:44.800
iPhones. No fancy editing, no fancy

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.479
equipment, just talking to the camera,

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.479
showing what they were doing. Hey, we're

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:50.319
building a property. We want to document

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:53.360
our journey. And this guy built an

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:55.280
Instagram following of 130,000 people

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.680
just showing them what the process was

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:59.440
like of dreaming, building, planning,

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.360
trying to raise money for this

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.199
short-term rental property. By the time

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.520
he was actually ready to launch, he had

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:08.240
tons of people ready and willing to give

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.400
him money to launch his project or to be

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.639
his first customers at his project to go

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:15.360
stay in his property. If you bring the

00:08:12.639 --> 00:08:16.960
world along with you through these free

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.879
media platforms like Facebook,

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.720
Instagram, Tik Tok, whatever, then you

00:08:18.879 --> 00:08:22.720
can skip the line. The downside to this

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.039
is it's public accountability. You might

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:26.720
fail. You might feel embarrassed if it

00:08:25.039 --> 00:08:28.479
doesn't go well. Maybe the algorithm

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.479
never picks up your videos and you feel

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:32.399
like it was a waste of time. But this is

00:08:30.479 --> 00:08:34.800
an asymmetric bet. There's a lot more

00:08:32.399 --> 00:08:36.320
upside to posting free videos on the

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.640
internet, spending a little bit of time

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.159
doing so, than there is downside. Now,

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.640
one of the most valuable things you can

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:44.480
do is to start following a bunch of

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.839
people that are doing this on Instagram.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.080
What are they doing? What are their

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:49.920
hooks? What background music are they

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.000
using? How often are they posting? What

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.000
days are they posting? What type of

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.959
asset are they building or buying? And

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.920
what do the comments say? Right? Open up

00:08:56.959 --> 00:09:02.320
Instagram in your browser and copy all

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:04.000
the comments, paste them in the chat GPT

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:06.240
and say, "What are some trends or themes

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.320
found in these comments that I can learn

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.240
when making my short form videos that

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:11.839
are in the same niche as this guy's?"

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.600
Maybe all the comments refer to the fact

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:15.040
that he had to quit his job to do this.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.560
Well, if you're quitting your job to do

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.839
this, then you should talk about that in

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.519
your content a lot, too, because it

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:20.720
clearly resonates with people. Maybe

00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:22.320
people in the comments are just talking

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.080
about how cute their dog is in the

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.600
background of the video. Maybe you need

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.839
a dog in the background of your video.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.320
I'm serious. Getting a like on social

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:33.360
media takes a millisecond, but getting a

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:35.279
comment takes seconds. 5 to 50 plus

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.519
